    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1001">

                <pb facs="0967" n="709" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg709"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 841.731/396</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Page</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 904]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-02-15">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">February 15, 1915</hi>.</date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received February 27</hi>.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to report that Sir Edward
                    Grey has informed me that the search which the censors are making to ascertain
                    the reasons for stopping commercial telegrams brings this result, <pb
                        facs="0968" n="710" xml:id="pg710"/> viz., that of 60 or 70 telegrams
                    emanating from the United States and dated in November or December, they find
                    that only 3 were stopped by the British censors.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Walter Hines Page</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
